              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "The word **hosanna** has two primary, interconnected meanings depending on the context\u2014one is a modern cry of praise, and the other is an ancient plea for salvation. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Modern Definition\nIn contemporary English, **hosanna** is used as a noun or interjection representing:\n* **An exclamation of praise, adoration, or joy.** It is most commonly used in Christian and Jewish religious worship to express adoration for God or a savior. \n\n### 2. Literal & Historical Definition (Etymology)\nHistorically and etymologically, the word is not actually a declaration of praise (like *hallelujah*), but a **plea for rescue.** \n* **Hebrew Origin:** It comes from the Hebrew phrase *Hoshi\u2019a na* (\u05d4\u05d5\u05b9\u05e9\u05b4\u05c1\u05d9\u05e2\u05b8\u05d4\u05be\u05e0\u05b8\u05bc\u05d0). \n  * *Yasha* means \"to save\" or \"deliver\".\n  * *Na* is a particle of entreaty meaning \"please\" or \"now\".\n  * Therefore, the literal translation is **\"Save, please!\"** or **\"Save us now!\"**.\n\n---\n\n### Context in Religious Traditions\n\n#### In Judaism\nIn the Hebrew Bible, the phrase appears in Psalm 118:25: *\"Save us, we pray, O Lord!\"* Over time, this plea became a liturgical formula. During the Jewish festival of Sukkot (the Feast of Tabernacles), people wave palm and willow branches while reciting *Hoshanot*\u2014prayers requesting God's salvation and deliverance.\n\n#### In Christianity\nThe word took on a major shift during Jesus' Triumphal Entry into Jerusalem (celebrated as Palm Sunday). As Jesus rode into the city on a donkey, the crowds waved palm branches and shouted:\n> *\"Hosanna to the Son of David! Blessed is he who comes in the name of the Lord! Hosanna in the highest heaven!\"* (Matthew 21:9)\n\nIn this moment, the word bridged both meanings: \n1. It was a **prayer for deliverance**\u2014the people were crying out to Jesus to save them from Roman oppression.\n2. It was a **shout of praise and recognition**\u2014they were welcoming him as the promised Messiah (the Savior). \n\nToday, Christian liturgies (such as the Catholic Mass and various Protestant services) still use \"Hosanna\" during the *Sanctus* (\"Holy, Holy, Holy\") to joyfully praise Christ.",
